{
  "response": "# Verdict: REJECT\n\n## Findings\n\n**Overall Assessment**: This component fails fundamental accessibility requirements for interactive elements. It uses a non-interactive semantic element (`<div>`) for a toggle action, lacks keyboard support entirely, and omits all necessary ARIA attributes to communicate state to assistive technology. The design is functionally broken for keyboard and screen reader users.\n\n**Pre-commitment Predictions**:\n1.  **Semantic HTML violation**: Trigger uses `<div>` instead of `<button>`.\n2.  **Keyboard accessibility missing**: No `onKeyDown` handler for Enter/Space activation.\n3.  **ARIA attributes missing**: No `aria-expanded`, no `aria-controls`.\n4.  **Screen reader text issues**: The `+`/`-` symbols are exposed to AT, creating confusing announcements.\n5.  **Focus management gap**: No focus indicator styling (implied by lack of native button).\n\n**Critical Findings** (blocks access):\n\n1.  **Non-interactive element used for interactive action**\n    - **Evidence**: `BuggyExpandable.jsx:10` \u2014 `<div className=\"expandable-trigger\" onClick={toggle}>`\n    - **User group impacted**: Keyboard users, Screen reader users.\n    - **WCAG/APG Citation**: WCAG 2.1.1 (Keyboard), WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure Pattern.\n    - **Why this matters**: A `<div>` is not focusable by default and does not convey \"button\" semantics to assistive technology. Screen reader users will hear the title but won't know it's interactive. Keyboard users cannot reach or activate it.\n    - **Fix**: Replace `<div className=\"expandable-trigger\">` with `<button type=\"button\" className=\"expandable-trigger\">`.\n\n2.  **Missing keyboard activation support**\n    - **Evidence**: `BuggyExpandable.jsx:10-13` \u2014 Only `onClick` is present; no `onKeyDown` handler.\n    - **User group impacted**: Keyboard users.\n    - **WCAG/APG Citation**: WCAG 2.1.1 (Keyboard).\n    - **Why this matters**: Even if focusable, custom elements require explicit handling of Enter and Space keys to match native button behavior.\n    - **Fix**: Add `onKeyDown={(e) => { if (e.key === 'Enter' || e.key === ' ') { e.preventDefault(); toggle(); } }}` to the trigger element.\n\n3.  **Missing ARIA state communication**\n    - **Evidence**: `BuggyExpandable.jsx:10-14` \u2014 `isExpanded` state exists in React but is not reflected in the DOM via `aria-expanded`.\n    - **User group impacted**: Screen reader users.\n    - **WCAG/APG Citation**: WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value), WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure Pattern.\n    - **Why this matters**: Without `aria-expanded`, screen reader users cannot know if the content is currently visible or hidden. They also don't know what element controls the panel without `aria-controls`.\n    - **Fix**: Add `aria-expanded={isExpanded}` and `aria-controls=\"content-id\"` to the trigger. Ensure the content div has `id=\"content-id\"`.\n\n**Major Findings** (significantly degrades experience):\n\n4.  **Visual symbols exposed to assistive technology**\n    - **Evidence**: `BuggyExpandable.jsx:12` \u2014 `{isExpanded ? ' \u2212' : ' +'}` renders as text nodes.\n    - **User group impacted**: Screen reader users.\n    - **WCAG/APG Citation**: WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value).\n    - **Why this matters**: Screen readers will announce \"Title plus\" or \"Title minus\". This is redundant and confusing when `aria-expanded` is present. The symbols are purely visual indicators of state.\n    - **Fix**: Wrap the symbols in `<span aria-hidden=\"true\">{isExpanded ? ' \u2212' : ' +'}</span>`.\n\n5.  **Missing focus indicator styling**\n    - **Evidence**: `BuggyExpandable.jsx:10` \u2014 Using a `<div>` removes default browser focus rings. No CSS is shown to replace it.\n    - **User group impacted**: Keyboard users, Low vision users.\n    - **WCAG/APG Citation**: WCAG 2.4.7 (Focus Visible).\n    - **Why this matters**: If the element is made focusable (e.g., via `tabIndex` or changing to `<button>`), it requires a visible focus indicator that meets contrast requirements.\n    - **Fix**: Ensure CSS includes `:focus-visible` styles with sufficient contrast and outline width.\n\n**What's Missing** (gaps, unhandled edge cases, unstated assumptions):\n\n-   **Missing `aria-controls` pairing**: The trigger does not reference the content panel, breaking the programmatic relationship between control and controlled element.\n-   **Missing heading semantics**: If this component is used as a section header, it lacks `<h2>` or similar semantic structure.\n-   **Missing focus restoration design**: If this component is part of a larger form or modal, focus management upon expansion/collapse is undefined.\n\n**Multi-Perspective Notes**:\n\n-   **Screen reader user**: Hears the title text, followed by \"+\" or \"-\". No indication that it's clickable. No indication of expanded state. Cannot activate with keyboard.\n-   **Keyboard-only user**: Cannot tab to the trigger. Cannot activate it even if focused (no Enter/Space support).\n-   **Low vision user**: If focus styles are missing (implied), the focused element is invisible. The `+`/`-` symbols rely on visual contrast which may be lost in high-contrast modes if not styled correctly.\n-   **Cognitive accessibility**: The interaction model relies on visual cues (+/-) that aren't programmatically linked to state, making it harder for users who rely on consistent patterns or assistive technology.\n\n**Verdict Justification**:\nThe component fails at the foundational level of web accessibility: semantics and keyboard operability. It is a \"div\" masquerading as a button without the necessary ARIA attributes to bridge the gap. This is not a minor styling issue; it renders the component completely inaccessible to assistive technology users. The design must be revised to use native HTML elements and complete ARIA patterns before implementation.\n\n**Open Questions (unscored)**:\n-   Is this component intended to be part of an accordion group? If so, `aria-selected` or specific accordion roles may be needed instead of a simple disclosure pattern.\n-   Are there CSS styles elsewhere that provide focus indicators for `.expandable-trigger`? (Assumed no based on code snippet).\n\n### A11y Evidence Finding\nfinding_id: buggy-expandable-critical-failures\nfingerprint: 8a7b9c2d\nsource: BuggyExpandable.jsx lines 10-14\nwcag_or_apg: WCAG 2.1.1, WCAG 4.1.2, WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure Pattern\nsection_508_fpc_context: Not in scope (WCAG 2.2 AA focus)\nseverity: CRITICAL\nperspective_alarms: screen reader=HIGH, keyboard-only=HIGH, low vision=MEDIUM, cognitive=MEDIUM\nevidence: `<div className=\"expandable-trigger\" onClick={toggle}>` lacks semantic role and keyboard support. `isExpanded` state is not reflected in DOM.\nreproduction_steps: 1. Open component in browser. 2. Attempt to tab to trigger. 3. Attempt to activate with Enter/Space. 4. Inspect accessibility tree.\nexpected_behavior: Trigger should be a focusable button, activatable by keyboard, and announce expanded state to AT.\nactual_behavior: Trigger is an unfocusable div. No keyboard support. No ARIA state.\ntrend: new",
